content.text:
Real battle view: water% 42.7, foam 8.8%, lum 135 — plausible. Samples: center (65,130,148) teal water; left (105,113,102) ground green-ish; right (40,43,49) dark (maybe rock/shadow). Good.

One more consideration: performance — river plane now 25×71 = 1775 verts (fine); shader ~ 8 sin per pixel + 3 extra wave evaluations for normals (each waves() 4 sins) = 16 sins/pixel — on mobile at river coverage that's meaningful but acceptable? Mobile GPU: full-screen 42% of 2400×1400 (DS resolution?) could be ~1.4M pixels × 16 sin ≈ 22M sin/frame — mobile can do but maybe 20-30% frame cost. Mitigation: reduce normal derivative to 2 extra wave evals using cheaper single-wave normals? Compute analytic derivative instead of finite differences: derivative of each sin term analytically → same count of cos but no extra evaluations (saves 2/3). Implement analytic gradient:

For wave sum: h = Σ A_i sin(k_i·p + φ_i + t*w_i). dh/dx = Σ A_i k_ix cos(...). Add function `waves_grad(p,t) -> vec2` computing cos for the 4 terms (4 cos). Then n = normalize(vec3(-grad.x*0.85, 1, -grad.y*0.85)). Total sin/cos per pixel: 4 sin (h) + 4 cos (grad) = 8 transcendentals vs current 16 sin. Better and smoother.

Let me implement analytic gradient, replacing finite difference. Keep wave amplitudes consistent: base function multiplies sum by 0.55; gradient accordingly A_i*0.55*k.

Rewrite shader portion: add
```glsl
vec2 waves_grad(vec2 p, float t) {
    vec2 g = vec2(0.0);
    vec2 d1 = vec2(0.80, -0.60); float k1 = 1.35;
    g += cos(dot(p, d1) * k1 + t * 0.95) * d1 * k1;
    vec2 d2 = vec2(-0.62, -0.78); float k2 = 2.40;
    g += 0.55 * cos(dot(p, d2) * k2 - t * 1.30) * d2 * k2;
    vec2 d3 = vec2(0.97, 0.24); float k3 = 4.60;
    g += 0.28 * cos(dot(p, d3) * k3 + t * 2.00) * d3 * k3;
    vec2 d4 = vec2(-0.36, 0.93); float k4 = 8.20;
    g += 0.14 * cos(dot(p, d4) * k4 - t * 2.80) * d4 * k4;
    return g * 0.55;
}
```
Then fragment: 
```glsl
vec2 g = waves_grad(p, t);
vec3 n = normalize(vec3(-g.x * 0.85, 1.0, -g.y * 0.85));
```
Wait derivative magnitudes: k up to 8.2*0.14=1.15; sum ~1.35+1.5+1.4+1.15 ≈ 5.4 * 0.55 ≈ 3 → n.x up to 3 → slope strong ~70°. Scaling 0.85 keeps steep. Maybe scale 0.35 for gentler ripples. Use 0.45.

Also h0 needed for roughness: keep waves() call (4 sin) → 4 sin + 4 cos = 8 ✓.

Edit and re-parse, quick render compare stats (should remain similar).

