void fragment(){
  vec2 p = UV; // 0..1
  // 圆角矩形 SDF
  float w = 0.02;  // 边框宽
  vec2 d = abs(p - 0.5) - vec2(0.5 - 0.02, 0.5 - 0.10);
  float sdf = length(max(d, 0.0)) + min(max(d.x, d.y), 0.0) - 0.10; // 圆角
  if (sdf > 0.0) discard;
  ...
}
```
Complex but doable. Simpler: draw backing rect with rounded corners via smoothstep, fill portion, top highlight line, border stroke, tick marks at 0.25/0.5/0.75, and a lag tail. I'll write carefully.

Actually simpler & robust: use two quads: backing quad with shader (rounded rect dark + border + subtle gloss), and fill quad with shader (rounded fill gradient + highlight + edge glow), where fill quad's scale.x animates. But rounded fill with changing width needs UV math on the sub-quad → the fill's rounded corners would scale oddly. Instead single-quad shader computing everything from `hp` uniform is cleaner.

Let me write the bar shader:

